      "content": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Small unitary-valued maps · Alec Kriebel</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"An explicit Lipschitz-nonincreasing deformation answering Gromov's question 24(i), with a four-page proof, independent audit reports, and reproducible checks.\">\n<meta name=\"author\" content=\"Alec Kriebel\"><meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"A Lipschitz-nonincreasing deformation of small unitary-valued maps\">\n<meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"A four-page proof, a documented priority audit, and reproducible verification companions.\">\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://aleckriebel.github.io/Math/papers/gromov-unitary-lipschitz/\">\n<link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/svg+xml\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'%3E%3Crect width='32' height='32' rx='7' fill='%231c3e48'/%3E%3Ctext x='16' y='24' text-anchor='middle' font-family='Georgia' font-size='26' fill='%23fffaf0'%3EU%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E\">\n<style>\n:root{color-scheme:light;--ink:#19353e;--muted:#52666b;--paper:#fcfaf4;--line:#d9dfd8;--accent:#a14c29}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:var(--paper);color:var(--ink);font:17px/1.65 system-ui,-apple-system,sans-serif}a{color:inherit;text-underline-offset:4px}a:hover{color:var(--accent)}a:focus-visible{outline:3px solid var(--accent);outline-offset:5px}.wrap{max-width:1080px;margin:auto;padding:0 28px}nav{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:24px;padding:25px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-size:14px}nav a{text-decoration:none}.eyebrow{font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:var(--accent)}header{padding:66px 0 42px;max-width:930px}h1,h2{font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-weight:400;line-height:1.12}h1{font-size:clamp(36px,5.5vw,62px);margin:18px 0 25px;letter-spacing:-.035em}h2{font-size:32px;margin:0 0 20px}h3{font-size:17px;line-height:1.35}p{margin:0 0 18px}.intro{font-size:21px;max-width:800px}.byline{font-size:14px;color:var(--muted)}.buttons{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;margin:26px 0 0}.button{display:inline-block;border:1px solid var(--ink);padding:10px 17px;text-decoration:none;font-size:14px;font-weight:650}.button.primary{background:var(--ink);color:var(--paper)}.button:hover{border-color:var(--accent)}.status{border-top:1px solid var(--line);border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);padding:16px 0;font-size:14px;color:var(--muted)}section{padding:44px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}.two{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.5fr 1fr;gap:55px}.formula{font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:clamp(18px,2.7vw,28px);line-height:1.5;padding:20px 0;overflow-x:auto;white-space:nowrap}.formula small{font-size:17px}aside{border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding-left:24px;font-size:15px}.steps{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:30px}.number{color:var(--accent);font:26px Georgia,serif}ul,ol{padding-left:22px}li{margin-bottom:9px}.quiet{color:var(--muted);font-size:14px}code{font:14px/1.7 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,monospace}pre{background:#edf0e9;padding:15px;overflow:auto}.files{list-style:none;padding:0}.files li{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:20px;padding:12px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}.files span{color:var(--muted);font-size:13px;flex-shrink:0}footer{padding:30px 0 50px;color:var(--muted);font-size:13px}.skip{position:absolute;top:-100px}.skip:focus{top:10px;background:white;padding:10px}details{margin-top:18px}summary{cursor:pointer;text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:4px}@media(max-width:700px){.wrap{padding:0 20px}header{padding-top:42px}.two,.steps{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:28px}aside{padding-left:18px}.intro{font-size:19px}section{padding:33px 0}.files li{display:block}.files span{display:block;margin-top:4px}nav{font-size:13px}.formula{font-size:19px}}\n</style>\n</head>\n<body><a class=\"skip\" href=\"#result\">Skip to result</a><div class=\"wrap\">\n<nav aria-label=\"Primary navigation\"><a href=\"../../\">Alec Kriebel / Research</a><a href=\"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/main/gromov_unitary_lipschitz_contraction\">Source &amp; audit reports ↗</a></nav>\n<header><p class=\"eyebrow\">Geometry · Gromov question [?24](i)</p><h1>A Lipschitz-nonincreasing deformation of small unitary-valued maps</h1><p class=\"intro\">An explicit deformation to constant maps, with a Lipschitz bound at every step. The proof fits in four pages.</p><p class=\"byline\">Alec Kriebel · <a href=\"https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X\">ORCID</a> · Version 1.1 · 23 September 2026</p><div class=\"buttons\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"paper.pdf\">Read the paper · PDF</a><a class=\"button\" href=\"reproducibility.zip\">Download verification package</a></div></header>\n<div class=\"status\"><strong>Verification:</strong> analytic proof passed independent AI-assisted algebraic and geometric audits. This is an unrefereed preprint. The priority search found no earlier explicit statement of this result; first priority is not established.</div>\n<main><section id=\"result\" class=\"two\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">The result</p><h2>One formula. Every dimension.</h2><p>Fix a point <i>p</i> on the unit-round sphere. For a map Φ into U(N), write <i>A</i> = Φ(<i>p</i>) and <i>W</i> = <i>A</i><sup>*</sup>Φ. When Lip(Φ) ≤ ½, set</p><div class=\"formula\" aria-label=\"H sub t of Phi equals A times W plus t I times the inverse of I plus t W\"><i>H</i><sub>t</sub>(Φ) = <i>A</i> (<i>W</i> + <i>tI</i>)(<i>I</i> + <i>tW</i>)<sup>−1</sup>.</div><p>The homotopy starts at Φ, ends at the constant Φ(<i>p</i>), and fixes every constant map. It depends continuously on the entire input map and remains in the original U(N).</p><div class=\"formula\">Lip(<i>H</i><sub>t</sub>(Φ)) ≤ <span style=\"display:inline-flex;vertical-align:middle;flex-direction:column;text-align:center;font-size:.85em;line-height:1.3\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid;padding:0 8px\">1 − <i>t</i><sup>2</sup></span><span>1 + <i>t</i><sup>2</sup></span></span> Lip(Φ), <small>0 ≤ <i>t</i> ≤ 1.</small></div></div><aside><h3>Exact scope</h3><p>The target uses either the unnormalized Hilbert–Schmidt Riemannian distance or the operator-norm length distance. Both strict and closed Lipschitz bounds are preserved.</p><p>This answers the literal threshold ½ question on page 36 of <a href=\"https://www.ihes.fr/~gromov/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/101-problemsOct1-2017.pdf\">Gromov’s 2017 problem list</a>, catalogued as <a href=\"https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/AMR-066-0025\">AMR-066-0025</a>.</p><p>The constant depends on Φ. The theorem does not contract U(N) itself to a single prescribed point.</p></aside></section>\n<section><p class=\"eyebrow\">The proof in three steps</p><div class=\"steps\"><div><span class=\"number\">01</span><h3>Confine the spectrum</h3><p>The diameter π and the bound ½ place every eigenvalue of the normalized image in the closed right semicircle.</p></div><div><span class=\"number\">02</span><h3>Control the inverse</h3><p>A positive matrix inequality bounds (I + tW)<sup>−1</sup>. An ordered resolvent identity gives the contraction factor without assuming the matrices commute.</p></div><div><span class=\"number\">03</span><h3>Integrate along curves</h3><p>Apply the derivative bound to the image of a spherical geodesic. This proves the intrinsic Lipschitz estimate, including for nonsmooth maps.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"two\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Check it yourself</p><h2>Proof first. Small scripts alongside.</h2><p>The analytic argument is self-contained apart from standard finite-dimensional spectral and curve-length facts. No numerical computation is required.</p><p>The package includes <strong>10 exact algebra checks</strong> and <strong>7,679 deterministic numerical checks</strong>, all passing. They cover noncommuting boundary matrices, both norms, endpoints, and failure outside the semicircle.</p><p>From the extracted reproducibility archive:</p><pre><code>python3 verification/verify_exact.py\n# Optional: requires NumPy\npython3 verification/verify_numeric.py</code></pre><p class=\"quiet\">Finite diagnostics are not a formal proof of the theorem or a substitute for its continuity and intrinsic-length arguments.</p></div><div><ul class=\"files\"><li><a href=\"paper.pdf\">Four-page paper</a><span>PDF</span></li><li><a href=\"reproducibility.zip\">Source, scripts &amp; review reports</a><span>ZIP</span></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/main/gromov_unitary_lipschitz_contraction/audit/verification_summary.md\">Verification checklist</a><span>Read online</span></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/main/gromov_unitary_lipschitz_contraction/audit/preprint_readiness.md\">Fresh preprint reviews &amp; readiness</a><span>Read online</span></li><li><a href=\"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/blob/main/gromov_unitary_lipschitz_contraction/audit/priority_review.md\">Documented priority audit</a><span>Read online</span></li><li><a href=\"zenodo-upload.zip\">Ready-to-use Zenodo upload kit</a><span>ZIP</span></li><li><a href=\"zenodo-metadata.json\">Zenodo metadata</a><span>JSON</span></li><li><a href=\"SHA256SUMS.txt\">Download checksums</a><span>SHA-256</span></li></ul></div></section>\n<section class=\"two\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">Context &amp; priority</p><h2>An elementary quantitative answer.</h2><p>The Cayley and Möbius transformations are classical. A standard logarithm chart already gives contraction to constants when no Lipschitz bound is required during the deformation. The contribution presented here is the explicit quantitative deformation preserving the stated sublevels.</p><p>The audit compared the construction with earlier matrix-transform and unitary-group geometry literature. No earlier explicit resolution or exact quantitative retraction was located. The search does not exclude folklore, unindexed work, or results under other terminology.</p></div><div><h3>Version 1.1 deposited on Zenodo</h3><p>The <a href=\"https://zenodo.org/records/22929857\">public Zenodo record</a> contains the reviewed paper and reproducibility archive. Zenodo assigns DOI <code>10.5281/zenodo.22929857</code>. The direct record link works; DOI resolution was still pending at the deposit audit. The download kit preserves the pre-deposit preparation snapshot.</p><p class=\"quiet\">Generative AI assisted the candidate proof, independent internal checks, literature search, and writing. Paper and prose: CC BY 4.0. Code: MIT. See the package for details.</p></div></section></main>\n<footer>© 2026 Alec Kriebel · <a href=\"../../\">Research index</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/tree/main/gromov_unitary_lipschitz_contraction\">Complete project record</a></footer>\n</div></body></html>\n",
